Assumption #1: A point is a zero dimensional, flat object.

Euclid assumes that the point is dimensionless and curvature-free. This assumption is not derived; it is declared. A curvature-free seed layer implies that no geometric information is inherited from the base metric. As a result, the transition from 0D to 1D requires the spontaneous emergence of extension. This violates structural inheritance: a collapsed metric cannot broadcast dimensional growth, and something cannot be built upon nothing.  

Mathematically, the collapse is obscured by the convention 𝑟^0 = 1. This identity is algebraic, not geometric. It applies to all values of 𝑟, including the collapsed “measurement” 𝑟 = 0; it is treated as describing a 0D metric. For our purposes, no simplification will take place. Euclid’s geometric definition of the point requires 𝑟 = 0, and a true collapsed metric would satisfy 𝑟^0 = 1. If a collapsed metric evaluates to 0^0 = 1, then the metric did not collapse into spontaneous nothingness; whatever the remainder is, is the metric; and that remainder is curvature. In other words, how many sets of a collapsed radius does it take to make a collapsed radius? One. To Euclid, it's zero. This is the source to most pathologies in modern mathematics. Euclid saw zero as the geometric definition of nothingness. This is not the case. Space nor does anything "spontaneous generate"; it emerges. To emerge, the very definition is to have been produced by... something, not nothing.

