2.2.2 Logical Fallacy B2  
On top of the primitive fallacies already established, Euclid’s infinite line introduces an additional contradiction. We accept that the universe has a closed structure; we accept that for anything to be built, it must have a substrate on which to exist. For a line to extend infinitely, the only logical conclusion is that it must breach the universe’s construct and that outside of its construct lies the same substrate.  
What lies beyond our universe? Do we possess any implicit or explicit empirical evidence of a substrate outside its boundary? We do not. Without knowing whether the substrate beyond the universe equals the substrate within it, one cannot assume that an infinite line can continuously project in the same trajectory beyond the universe’s border. An infinite line requires an infinite substrate. If no substrate exists beyond the universe’s boundary, then dimensional conditions dictate that the infinite line terminates at that boundary… or is deflected by a different substrate in a limitless number of ways.  
The only alternative is to claim that the line itself dictates dimension, an inversion of structural hierarchy that violates the foundational rule that objects inherit their dimensional conditions from the substrate they occupy. A geometric object cannot outrun, outgrow, or override the dimensional constraints of the universe that contains it. Therefore, the infinite line is not a geometric object; it is a logical impossibility.  
